<DocPage
  title="Privacy Policy: Open Citadel"
  description="How Open Citadel handles your data: what stays on your device, what your account holds, and what is sent when you use Samwell Cloud and Compass."
  eyebrow="Legal"
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  updated={lastUpdated}
  sections={sections}
>
  <p>
    Open Citadel is built on a simple idea: your books, notes, and
    thinking are yours. This policy explains what data the app uses,
    where it lives, and what leaves your device when you choose to use
    Samwell Cloud.
  </p>

  <section id="who-we-are">
    <h2>
      Who We Are
    </h2>
    <p>
      Open Citadel is developed by <strong
        >Thamsanqa Ncube</strong
      >, an individual developer publishing on Google Play and the App
      Store. There is no separate company at this time. If that changes,
      this policy will be updated to name the responsible entity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>
      Summary
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your library, highlights, notes, chats, goals, and daily logs are
        stored in a local database on your device. We do not keep a copy
        of them on our servers.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can read, highlight, and use on-device Samwell without an
        account.
      </li>
      <li>
        Samwell Cloud and Compass need a plan. You can buy one with or
        without an account. When you use them, what you send and the
        context Samwell needs to answer is passed through our server to an
        AI provider to generate a reply.
      </li>
      <li>
        Subscriptions are paid through Google Play or the App Store. We
        never see your payment details.
      </li>
      <li>
        We do not sell data or show ads, and the app has no advertising
        SDKs. If you choose to, you can share how the app performs
        (Settings, Share app performance) to help us make it faster. It is
        off until you turn it on.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="on-device">
    <h2>
      What Stays On Your Device
    </h2>
    <p>
      Everything you create or add in Open Citadel is stored in a local
      SQLite database inside the app's private storage. That includes your
      books, reading progress, highlights, notes, collections, chat
      history with Samwell, Compass goals, and daily logs.
    </p>
    <p>
      On-device Samwell runs an AI model on your phone. Conversations with
      it never leave your device.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading aloud uses your phone's own voices, or the Enhanced voices,
      which are downloaded once and run on your phone. Cloud voices are
      described under <a href="#samwell-cloud"
        >Samwell Cloud &amp; Compass</a
      >.
    </p>
    <p>
      Posts you open from blogs are saved on your phone so you can
      highlight them and ask Samwell about them. A post you only opened is
      removed 30 days after you last read it; ones you queued, favourited,
      highlighted, noted or chatted about stay until you remove them
      (Remove Saved Copy) or uninstall.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="account">
    <h2>
      Your Account
    </h2>
    <p>
      You can sign in or create an account to use Samwell Cloud and
      Compass. Sign-in is handled by Logto, open-source sign-in software
      that we run on our own server, so your account details are not
      shared with Logto or anyone else. Your account holds your email address and, if you give one, your
      name. The app keeps your sign-in session in your device's secure
      storage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signing out ends the session on your device. It does not delete
      anything stored on your device.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Buying without an account.</strong> If
      you buy a plan without signing in, the app makes a random identifier
      and secret for this device and keeps them in its secure storage. We
      keep only that identifier and a hash of the secret, with your plan,
      so the purchase works on this device. We never learn a name or
      email. If you later make an account, the purchase moves to it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="samwell-cloud">
    <h2>
      Samwell Cloud &amp; Compass
    </h2>
    <p>
      Samwell Cloud answers through our server instead of running on your
      phone. Compass, the part of the app for goals, daily logs, and
      check-ins, works with Samwell Cloud.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you send Samwell Cloud a message, the app sends our server:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your message and the rest of that conversation;</li>
      <li>
        a short summary of your journey: books you are reading or have
        finished, the themes you highlight most, and your active goal,
        what you track for it, and how often you have logged lately;
      </li>
      <li>
        anything Samwell looks up in the app to answer you, such as a
        passage from a book, your highlights and notes, or your goal
        history and logs.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The same applies when the app asks Samwell Cloud to suggest a title
      for a chat, suggest tags for a highlight, or write a takeaway when a
      goal ends, and to the optional setup conversation where Samwell
      introduces the app and helps you find free books.
    </p>
    <p>Some features send more, only when you use them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What Samwell knows.</strong> A short
        profile of you that Samwell keeps on your phone, with your recent
        wins and open commitments, is sent with each cloud message so he
        can remember you. You can see and change it in Settings, What
        Samwell knows. His notes about a conversation are written on our
        server and sent back to your phone; the server keeps no copy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Web search.</strong> When Samwell
        searches the web, his search goes through OpenRouter to its search
        provider (Exa), and our server may fetch a page from those results
        to read it. Only the search terms leave, not your reading.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloud voices.</strong> When you
        listen in a cloud voice, the text being read is sent through our
        server and OpenRouter to the voice's provider to be spoken, and is
        not kept. The audio you have heard is kept on your phone so going
        back costs nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Podcast transcripts.</strong> When
        you ask for a transcript, the episode's web address (not the audio)
        is sent through our server and OpenRouter to ElevenLabs, which
        fetches the audio from the show and writes it out. The transcript
        waits on our server until your phone collects it, and is deleted
        from the server within about a day. It is kept on your phone.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Our server passes this to <a
          href="https://openrouter.ai/privacy"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenRouter</a
        >,
      which routes it to the AI model provider that generates the reply.
      OpenRouter's privacy policy and the policy of that model provider
      apply to how they handle it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our server does not store the content of your conversations. It
      stores what it needs to run your plan: your account ID, your plan,
      your credit balance, and a usage record for each request (the model
      used, the number of tokens, the cost, and the time). Like most
      servers, it also keeps technical logs of requests.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="performance">
    <h2>
      App Performance (Off Unless You Turn It On)
    </h2>
    <p>
      In Settings, "Share app performance" lets the app send how it
      performed, so slow and broken parts can be found and fixed. It is off
      until you turn it on, and you can turn it off at any time in the same
      place. Nothing recorded while it is off is ever sent.
    </p>
    <p>When it is on, the app sends:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        How long the app takes to open, each screen to be ready, and
        Samwell to answer, with which AI model answered and whether Samwell
        runs on your phone or in the cloud.
      </li>
      <li>
        How long other work takes, such as refreshing feeds, opening a post
        or a book, importing a book, collecting a transcript, and buying a
        plan, and whether it worked.
      </li>
      <li>
        Errors the app ran into, with web addresses, file names and email
        addresses removed from their messages, and on Android, the reason
        the app last closed when the system ended it (for example, to free
        memory).
      </li>
      <li>
        Your device's model, operating system and version, language,
        network and battery state, and the app's version and build.
      </li>
      <li>
        Which screens were opened, by their type (for example "a blog's
        page"), without the book, post, address or search they were opened
        with.
      </li>
      <li>The web address of the slowest network request while the app opens.</li>
      <li>
        A random identifier for this installation of the app, and your IP
        address, from which an approximate country is worked out.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It never includes what you read, highlight, write or say to Samwell,
      your name, your email or your account.
    </p>
    <p>
      This data is processed for us by <a
        href="https://expo.dev/privacy"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Expo (650 Industries, Inc.)</a
      >, the company whose app platform Open Citadel is built on, as our
      service provider. It is used only to find and fix problems in the
      app. It is not combined with data from other apps, used for
      advertising or shared with data brokers. Expo deletes it after 90
      days, in monthly batches, so a record can last up to 120 days.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="subscriptions">
    <h2>
      Subscriptions
    </h2>
    <p>
      Samwell Cloud plans are monthly subscriptions bought through Google
      Play or the App Store, which process the payment. We never see or
      store your card or payment details.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use <a
          href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">RevenueCat</a
        > to
      manage subscriptions. RevenueCat receives your account ID and your
      purchase history from the store so we can tell which plan you are
      on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>
      Permissions We Request
    </h2>
    <p>The Android app requests the following:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Storage</strong>: to import books
        you pick into your library and to save images, such as highlight
        cards, to your device. Files are only accessed when you choose
        them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Internet</strong>: to sign in, to
        use Samwell Cloud and Compass, to manage subscriptions, and to
        download free books and on-device AI models you choose.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On iOS, the app only reaches files you open or share into it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="third-parties">
    <h2>
      Third-Party Services
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Logto</strong>: sign-in and
        accounts, run on our own server.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenRouter</strong> and the AI
        model providers it routes to: generating Samwell Cloud replies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>RevenueCat</strong>: managing
        subscriptions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Play and the App Store</strong
        >: processing payments, under <a
          href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's</a
        >
        and <a
          href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple's</a
        > own
        privacy policies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hugging Face</strong>: hosting the
        on-device AI models you choose to download. These are plain file
        downloads with no personal data attached.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Project Gutenberg</strong>: the
        source of the free public domain books the app can find and
        download for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Expo (650 Industries, Inc.)</strong>:
        delivers app updates and, if you turn on Share app performance,
        processes the performance data described above.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hetzner</strong>: hosts our server
        (Samwell Cloud, accounts and plans).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ElevenLabs</strong>: writes podcast
        transcripts you ask for, through OpenRouter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Exa</strong>: web searches Samwell
        makes, through OpenRouter.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Blogs and podcasts.</strong> Feeds,
      posts, artwork and episode audio are fetched by your phone directly
      from each publisher's site, so those sites see your IP address, as
      they would in a browser. A blog's whole post is fetched from its site
      only if you choose to read that blog in the reader.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Note: this website (the page you're reading now) uses Vercel Web
      Analytics to count anonymous, aggregated page views. This is
      separate from the app and does not use cookies or collect personal
      data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention">
    <h2>
      Data Retention &amp; Deletion
    </h2>
    <p>
      Your library, notes, chats, goals, and logs live on your device. You
      can delete books, highlights, notes, chats, and goals in the app, or
      remove everything by uninstalling Open Citadel. We have no copy to
      keep.
    </p>
    <p>
      To delete your account and the records our server holds about it
      (your plan, credit balance, and usage records), open the app, go to
      Settings, and use <strong>DELETE ACCOUNT</strong>
      on the Cloud Account card. It takes effect immediately. If you no
      longer have the app, email us at the address below from the email on
      your account and we will do it within 30 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cancel any active subscription in Google Play or the App Store
      first, since deleting your account does not cancel it. See <a
        href="/delete-account">Delete Your Account</a
      > for the full steps and exactly what is deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Performance data is kept by Expo for up to 120 days and then deleted.
      It cannot be linked back to you, so it cannot be looked up for
      deletion; turning Share app performance off stops any more being
      sent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="children">
    <h2>
      Children's Privacy
    </h2>
    <p>
      Open Citadel is not directed at children under 13, and we do not
      knowingly collect personal information from children.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <h2>
      Changes To This Policy
    </h2>
    <p>
      We may update this policy as the app changes. The "Last updated"
      date at the top of this page always shows the latest revision, and
      important changes will be called out in the app's release notes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <h2>
      Contact
    </h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy or your data can be sent to <a
        href="mailto:thamsanqa.dev@gmail.com">thamsanqa.dev@gmail.com</a
      >. The rules for using the app are in
      the <a href="/terms"
        >Terms of Service</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocPage>
